meta {
  name: Log Activity (Invalid Type)
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/activities
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "leadId": "{{hotLeadId}}",
    "type": "sold"
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses a system-generated type", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /activities with `type: "sold"` — `400`

  **The security-relevant negative test.** Kept in the collection as executable
  documentation of a boundary that is easy to widen by accident.

  `ACTIVITY_TYPES` — the vocabulary the timeline *reads* — contains
  `lead_created`, `quoted`, `sold` and `audit_resolved`. `LOGGABLE_ACTIVITY_TYPES`
  — what a client may *write* — contains only `call`, `text`, `email` and `note`.

  The gap is deliberate. Those four are system-generated provenance, each written
  by the pipeline that owns the event. A client able to post a `sold` row could
  invent a sale that would appear on the **Sold scorecard** and the
  **Leaderboard**, both of which read from the same collections.

  If this request ever returns `201`, the write vocabulary has been widened and
  the dashboard's numbers are forgeable.

  `lead_created`, `quoted` and `audit_resolved` are rejected for the same reason.

  ## Response `400`
  ```json
  {
    "message": "Validation failed",
    "errors": { "fieldErrors": { "type": ["Invalid option: expected one of \"call\"|\"text\"|\"email\"|\"note\""] } }
  }
  ```
}
